    "product_id": "c78f9a49-2ab8-4376-961a-abb0efd8c68f",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 150740\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1240 AM PDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts will continue through the \n  week with dry conditions and near-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Monday.\n\nTightened pressure gradients across the region will continue to \nbring gusty south-southwesterly winds to the forecast area in the \nafternoons thanks to an upper-level trough across the Pacific \nNorthwest and a ridge to our east. Expect gust speeds generally \nbetween 25 and 35 mph, though can't rule out 40 mph speeds in \nisolated instances and/or in the higher terrain. Ensembles \ncontinue to show a reinforcing closed low riding along the \nCalifornia Coast before pushing into the southern Great Basin mid-\nto-late week. The resultant instability coupled with 0.75 to 1.25\ninches of PWAT over our eastern zones have resulted in increased \nPoPs across southeastern Lincoln and northern and eastern Mohave \ncounties Wednesday and Thursday. Additionally, WPC has painted a \nMarginal Excessive Rainfall Outlook over northern Mohave County \nfor Wednesday and Thursday. Coverage is expected to be isolated, \nbut storms that form have the potential to be efficient rain- \nmakers. For locations west of Interstate 15, expect mostly dry \nconditions and temperatures near-normal heading into the next work\nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Light \nsouthwest winds tonight give way to breezy south-southeast winds \nTuesday morning and afternoon. Some uncertainty exists in the exact \nwind direction and magnitude during the morning hours (15z-19z). \nThink on average, winds during this time will be from ~160, but \nwouldn't be surprised to see periods as far east as 120. By mid-\nafternoon, expecting a gradual turn to a more true south direction. \nGusts 15-25 knots likely (90%), with 30+ knots possible (25%). Winds \nshould subside after sunset and remain from the south-southwest \novernight. Mostly clear skies with just a few mid-level afternoon \nclouds. Temps peak just shy of 100F on Tuesday.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Breezy south winds \nlinger tonight in areas southeast of I-15, but elsewhere light winds \nare expected. Southerly to southeasterly winds pick up across most \nof the region Tuesday morning and persist through sunset. Peak gusts \nof 15-30 knots are likely (90%). Isolated convection possible (10-\n20%) across northwestern Arizona and eastern Lincoln County Tuesday \nafternoon/evening. Here, few to scattered clouds around 10kft are \nexpected, but mostly clear skies farther west.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat \nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
